{"name":"Cassia Buds","hex":"#e0cdda","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cassia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The spice made from the bark of members of the genus Cinnamomum other than true cinnamon (C. verum), when they are distinguished from cinnamon.","Such trees themselves, particularly the Chinese cinnamon, Cinnamomum cassia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cassia"},{"term":"buds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of bud"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of bud"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buds"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cinnamomum cassia","blocks":[{"text":"Cinnamomum cassia, called Chinese cassia, cassia cinnamon, or Chinese cinnamon, is an evergreen tree originating in southern China and widely cultivated there and elsewhere in South and Southeast Asia. It is one of several species of Cinnamomum used primarily for its aromatic bark, which is used as a spice. The buds are also used as a spice, especially in India, and were used by the ancient Romans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Cinnamomum_aromaticum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-039_cropped.jpg/330px-Cinnamomum_aromaticum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-039_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamomum_cassia","wikidata":"Q204148"},{"title":"Bud","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a bud is an undeveloped or embryonic shoot and normally occurs in the axil of a leaf or at the tip of a stem. Once formed, a bud may remain for some time in a dormant condition, or it may form a shoot immediately. Buds may be specialized to develop flowers or short shoots or may have the potential for general shoot development. The term bud is also used in zoology, where it refers to an outgrowth from the body which can develop into a new individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg/330px-Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bud","wikidata":"Q189838"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/CassiaEssentialOil.png/500px-CassiaEssentialOil.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/CassiaEssentialOil.png/500px-CassiaEssentialOil.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cassia (C. cassia) essential oil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACassiaEssentialOil.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Boym-durian-Lach-and-Kley-3-4-369.png/500px-Boym-durian-Lach-and-Kley-3-4-369.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Boym-durian-Lach-and-Kley-3-4-369.png/500px-Boym-durian-Lach-and-Kley-3-4-369.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"C. cassia (top left) depicted by Michał Boym (1655)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoym-durian-Lach-and-Kley-3-4-369.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Cassia_bark.jpg/330px-Cassia_bark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Cassia_bark.jpg/330px-Cassia_bark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dried cassia bark","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACassia_bark.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg/500px-Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg?utm_s